---
import ParadoxLayout from '../layouts/ParadoxLayout.astro';
---

<ParadoxLayout slug="monty-hall-problem">
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
        You're a contestant on a game show. There are three closed doors: one hides a bag of money,
        the other two hide goats. You pick a door. Before opening it, the host, who knows what's
        behind every door, opens a <em>different</em> door to reveal a goat. You're then offered a
        choice: stick with your original door, or switch to the other one that's still closed.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
        Most people's gut says it doesn't matter. Two doors are left, so it's 50/50, right? It isn't.
        <strong>Switching wins about twice as often as staying.</strong> Play a few rounds below to see
        it for yourself, then flip on "show me the stats" to see exactly why.
    </p>

    <!-- Doors. Capped at max-w-xs per door (rather than the full grid-column width) so the artwork
         reads at roughly 65% of its earlier on-screen size -- a deliberate cut to how much vertical
         room the exhibit takes up, since each door's height is driven by its width at a fixed 2:3
         image aspect ratio.

         #mh-group-bar is deliberately a CHILD of #mh-doors-row (not a sibling positioned via an
         outer wrapper) and that row carries `relative` itself: the bar is `absolute` and its
         left/width are computed in JS from getBoundingClientRect() calls made relative to this
         same row element, so the bar's positioning parent and its measurement reference must be
         the same element. They previously lived one level apart (the row centered itself with
         max-w-*/mx-auto inside a separate `relative` wrapper the bar was positioned against),
         which left a gap between the row's left edge and the wrapper's left edge that silently
         shifted the bar off -- invisible whenever that gap happened to be zero (a full-width row),
         but wrong as soon as the row started centering itself. -->
    <div id="mh-doors-row" class="relative grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 md:gap-6 max-w-xs sm:max-w-sm md:max-w-md mx-auto">
        {[1, 2, 3].map((n) => (
            <div id={`mh-door-col-${n}`} class="flex flex-col items-center">
                <button
                    id={`mh-door-${n}`}
                    type="button"
                    aria-pressed="false"
                    class="mh-door w-full rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-slate-200 shadow-sm transition disabled:cursor-default enabled:cursor-pointer enabled:hover:shadow-md enabled:hover:-translate-y-0.5"
                >
                    <img id={`mh-door-${n}-img`} src="" alt={`Door ${n}, closed`} class="w-full h-auto block" />
                </button>
                <!-- "Your Pick" is appended (bold) by JS once this door is the player's choice. -->
                <span id={`mh-door-${n}-caption`} class="mt-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700">Door {n}</span>
                <span id={`mh-door-${n}-prob`} class="mt-1 text-center font-mono text-sm font-semibold"></span>
            </div>
        ))}
        <!-- Connecting bar that visually joins the "other two doors" (the 66.7% group) once a pick
             has been made in stats mode. Its left/width are set in JS to run between the horizontal
             centers of those two doors' "66.7%" labels (not the wider door artwork above them) --
             the picked door can be door 1, 2, or 3, so the pair being grouped is sometimes adjacent
             (e.g. 2+3) and sometimes split around the picked door (e.g. 1+3 when door 2 is picked),
             which a fixed-width CSS bar can't represent correctly. -->
        <div id="mh-group-bar" class="hidden absolute h-1.5 bg-rose-500 rounded-full" style="top: 100%; margin-top: 2px;"></div>
    </div>

    <p id="mh-message" class="mt-3 text-center p-3 bg-slate-50 text-slate-800 rounded-lg text-base md:text-lg min-h-[3.5rem] flex items-center justify-center" aria-live="polite"></p>

    <!-- Actions row: a 3-column grid so the two game-flow buttons land in the true visual
         center of the row (an empty column on the left balances the toggle's column on the
         right) while "show stats" still pins to the hard right. A plain flexbox with the
         toggle at ml-auto can't do this -- the buttons end up centered only within the
         leftover space, not the row as a whole.

         The two outer columns use minmax(<toggle's content width>, 1fr) rather than a bare
         1fr: a bare 1fr is shorthand for minmax(auto, 1fr), so the empty left column (whose
         content-width floor is 0) and the toggle column (whose floor is its label + switch,
         ~131px) end up unequal once the buttons in the middle claim their auto width -- 1fr
         only splits genuinely *leftover* space evenly, it doesn't equalize the tracks
         outright. Pinning both floors to (a little above) the toggle's own minimum keeps the
         two columns equal AND guarantees the toggle never gets squeezed narrower than its
         label, which would otherwise overlap the "Play Again" button as the row tightens.
         The label reads "Show Stats" rather than the longer "Show me the stats" specifically
         to keep this floor small enough that the buttons in the middle still have room and
         don't wrap. On narrow screens the columns stack instead, since a 3-up grid has no
         room to breathe at phone widths. -->
    <div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(8.5rem,1fr)_auto_minmax(8.5rem,1fr)] items-center gap-3">
        <div class="hidden sm:block" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3">
            <button
                id="mh-action-btn"
                type="button"
                disabled
                class="px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold shadow-sm transition-colors bg-slate-300 text-slate-500 enabled:bg-amber-500 enabled:text-white enabled:hover:bg-amber-600 disabled:cursor-not-allowed"
            >
                Reveal Goat
            </button>
            <button
                id="mh-play-again"
                type="button"
                disabled
                class="px-6 py-3 rounded-lg font-semibold shadow-sm transition-colors bg-slate-300 text-slate-500 enabled:bg-teal-600 enabled:text-white enabled:hover:bg-teal-700 disabled:cursor-not-allowed"
            >
                Play Again
            </button>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center sm:justify-end gap-3">
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 whitespace-nowrap">Show Stats</span>
            <label class="relative inline-flex items-center cursor-pointer">
                <input type="checkbox" id="mh-stats-toggle" class="sr-only peer">
                <div class="w-11 h-6 bg-slate-300 peer-checked:bg-amber-500 rounded-full transition-colors"></div>
                <div class="absolute left-1 top-1 w-4 h-4 bg-white rounded-full transition-transform peer-checked:translate-x-5"></div>
            </label>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Cumulative scoreboard: always visible, not gated on stats mode. Shown as wins-out-of-attempts
         (a conditional frequency) for each strategy rather than a bare win count -- the research this
         exhibit is built on found that framing produces the strongest effect on learning to switch. -->
    <div id="mh-stats-panel" class="mt-5 bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-100">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 mb-2 text-center">Your results so far</h4>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
                <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-teal-700 font-semibold mb-1">Stayed</div>
                <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800"><span id="mh-stay-wins">0</span> / <span id="mh-stay-count">0</span></div>
                <div class="text-sm text-slate-500">won <span id="mh-stay-pct">—</span></div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-rose-700 font-semibold mb-1">Switched</div>
                <div class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800"><span id="mh-switch-wins">0</span> / <span id="mh-switch-count">0</span></div>
                <div class="text-sm text-slate-500">won <span id="mh-switch-pct">—</span></div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center mt-3">
            <button id="mh-reset-stats" type="button" class="text-xs text-slate-400 underline hover:text-slate-600">
                Reset my results
            </button>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-4">
        This is the classic version of the problem: the host always opens a door you didn't pick,
        always reveals a goat, and never opens the door hiding the money.
    </p>

    <hr class="my-8 border-slate-200" />

    <!-- ======================================================================
         Part 2: 100-Trial Simulator
         Runs many independent rounds under "always stay" and "always switch" side
         by side, so a visitor can watch each strategy's win rate converge toward
         its true long-run value (1/3 vs. 2/3) instead of relying on a handful of
         personally-played rounds, which is far too small a sample to feel
         conclusive on its own.
         ====================================================================== -->
    <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Now Try 100 Trials at Once</h3>
    <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">
        One round only tells you what happened once. Below, a hundred independent games run
        side by side, half playing "always stay," half playing "always switch," so you can
        watch both win rates settle in around their true long-run values instead of taking a
        single result (or your memory of a few rounds) as the whole story.
    </p>

    <!-- Grids are capped at max-w-[220px] (roughly 10 cells at ~20px each) rather than
         stretching to the card's full width -- letting them go w-full made each cell scale
         up to 40-50px, which reads as a handful of large tiles rather than "100 small trial
         outcomes" and burned far more vertical space than the grid needed. The mockup this
         was ported from keeps each grid to a compact, fixed-feeling size regardless of how
         wide the surrounding card is; capping max-width (rather than setting a fixed width
         outright) keeps it responsive on narrow phone screens while staying small on desktop. -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <!-- STAY BOARD -->
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 rounded-xl p-4">
            <div class="flex justify-between items-center mb-3">
                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-teal-700 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-teal-600"></span> Stay Strategy
                    </h4>
                    <span class="text-xs text-slate-500">Never switch doors</span>
                </div>
                <div class="text-right">
                    <span class="text-xl font-black text-teal-700"><span id="mhs-stay-wins">0</span></span>
                    <span class="text-xs text-slate-500">/ <span id="mhs-total-trials">100</span> (<span id="mhs-stay-pct">0.0</span>%)</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <canvas id="mhs-stay-canvas" class="block mx-auto w-full max-w-[220px] border border-slate-200 rounded-lg bg-white" style="aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;"></canvas>
        </div>

        <!-- SWITCH BOARD -->
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-rose-100 rounded-xl p-4">
            <div class="flex justify-between items-center mb-3">
                <div>
                    <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-rose-700 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                        <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-rose-600"></span> Switch Strategy
                    </h4>
                    <span class="text-xs text-slate-500">Always switch doors</span>
                </div>
                <div class="text-right">
                    <span class="text-xl font-black text-rose-700"><span id="mhs-switch-wins">0</span></span>
                    <span class="text-xs text-slate-500">/ <span id="mhs-total-trials-2">100</span> (<span id="mhs-switch-pct">0.0</span>%)</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <canvas id="mhs-switch-canvas" class="block mx-auto w-full max-w-[220px] border border-slate-200 rounded-lg bg-white" style="aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 justify-center mt-3 text-xs text-slate-500">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block bg-emerald-500"></span>Won</div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block bg-slate-200"></span>Lost</div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2"><span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-sm inline-block bg-slate-100 border border-slate-200"></span>Not yet run</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Current trial status: what just happened, in plain language, not just the grid colors. -->
    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 rounded-xl p-3 mt-4 flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-between items-center gap-2 text-sm">
        <div id="mhs-status-text" class="text-slate-600 italic">
            Ready to run. Press "Step" or "Run All" below.
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4 text-slate-500 font-medium shrink-0 text-xs">
            <div>Stay door: <span id="mhs-status-stay" class="text-slate-700 font-bold">—</span></div>
            <div>Switch door: <span id="mhs-status-switch" class="text-slate-700 font-bold">—</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Progress bar -->
    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 rounded-lg px-3 py-2 mt-3 flex items-center gap-3">
        <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-slate-600 shrink-0">
            Trial <span id="mhs-trial-num" class="text-amber-600 font-bold">0</span> / <span id="mhs-total-trials-3">100</span>
        </span>
        <div class="w-full bg-slate-200 rounded-full h-1.5 overflow-hidden">
            <div id="mhs-progress-bar" class="bg-amber-500 h-full w-0 transition-all duration-100"></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Step / Run All / Reset -->
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mt-4">
        <button
            id="mhs-step-btn"
            type="button"
            class="px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg font-semibold shadow-sm transition-colors bg-teal-600 text-white hover:bg-teal-700 disabled:opacity-50 disabled:cursor-not-allowed"
        >
            Step
        </button>
        <button
            id="mhs-run-btn"
            type="button"
            class="px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg font-semibold shadow-sm transition-colors bg-amber-500 text-white hover:bg-amber-600 disabled:opacity-50 disabled:cursor-not-allowed"
        >
            Run All 100 Trials
        </button>
        <button
            id="mhs-reset-btn"
            type="button"
            class="px-4 py-2.5 rounded-lg text-sm font-semibold text-slate-500 underline hover:text-slate-700"
        >
            Reset
        </button>
    </div>

    <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-6">
        Inspired by the Monty Hall simulation on
        <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WhSjcF-qjXs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-purple-600 underline hover:text-purple-800">MythBusters</a>.
        Further reading:
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Hall_problem" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-purple-600 underline hover:text-purple-800">The Monty Hall Problem on Wikipedia</a>.
    </p>
</ParadoxLayout>

<style>
    /* Pulsing highlight borders used to draw attention to the currently-actionable
       control: the "Reveal Goat" button, and the two doors once a stay/switch
       choice is available. Kept as a CSS animation (rather than a Tailwind utility) since
       Tailwind's default `animate-pulse` fades opacity, not a border/ring color. */
    @keyframes mh-pulse-amber-kf {
        0%, 100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgba(217, 119, 6, 0.55); }
        50% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 6px rgba(217, 119, 6, 0); }
    }
    @keyframes mh-pulse-teal-kf {
        0%, 100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgba(13, 148, 136, 0.55); }
        50% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 6px rgba(13, 148, 136, 0); }
    }
    @keyframes mh-pulse-rose-kf {
        0%, 100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgba(225, 29, 72, 0.55); }
        50% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 6px rgba(225, 29, 72, 0); }
    }
    .mh-pulse-amber { animation: mh-pulse-amber-kf 1.6s ease-in-out infinite; }
    .mh-pulse-teal { animation: mh-pulse-teal-kf 1.6s ease-in-out infinite; }
    .mh-pulse-rose { animation: mh-pulse-rose-kf 1.6s ease-in-out infinite; }

    /* Respect reduced-motion preferences: keep the color cue (the ring) but drop the animation. */
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .mh-pulse-amber, .mh-pulse-teal, .mh-pulse-rose { animation: none; }
    }
</style>

<script>
    import '../scripts/paradoxes/monty-hall-problem.js';
    import '../scripts/paradoxes/monty-hall-simulator.js';
</script>
